{"kind":"FETCH","data":{"headers":{},"body":"{\"id\":7804353,\"site\":\"lawnsguide\",\"type\":\"lawn-care\",\"short_title\":\"lawn-care-schedule-southern-ontario\",\"language\":\"en\",\"published_time\":\"2026-07-29 00:17:25\",\"modified_time\":\"2026-07-29 00:17:25\",\"author\":\"Sarah Chen\",\"img\":\"https://s.alicdn.com/@sc02/kf/A5fa5952ba240444cbce213bce08ba7e2I.jpg\",\"title\":\"Lawn Care Schedule for Southern Ontario: A Complete Seasonal Guide\",\"description\":\"Follow this month-by-month lawn care schedule tailored for Southern Ontario. Get expert tips on mowing, fertilizing, aerating &amp; overseeding for a lush, healthy lawn.\",\"url\":\"/lawn-care/lawn-care-schedule-southern-ontario\",\"body\":\"<p>Southern Ontario's climate presents a unique set of challenges and opportunities for lawn care enthusiasts. With cold, snowy winters, humid summers, and unpredictable shoulder seasons, maintaining a lush, green lawn in this region requires a well-planned, seasonally adjusted approach. Whether you are tending to a Kentucky bluegrass lawn in Mississauga, a fescue blend in Hamilton, or a perennial ryegrass mix in the Niagara region, understanding the right timing for each lawn care task is essential for long-term success.</p>\\n\\n<p>This comprehensive lawn care schedule for Southern Ontario breaks down every critical task by season, giving you a clear roadmap to follow throughout the year. From spring green-up and summer stress management to fall renovation and winter dormancy preparation, each section provides actionable steps, product recommendations, and timing tips that will help you achieve the healthiest lawn on your block.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Spring Lawn Care in Southern Ontario (April to Early June)</h2>\\n\\n<p>Spring in Southern Ontario is a time of renewal, but it is also one of the most critical periods for setting the foundation of a healthy lawn. As soil temperatures gradually rise above 10°C and the snow finally retreats, your cool-season grass begins to wake from dormancy. The actions you take during these weeks will determine how well your turf performs for the rest of the growing season.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Early Spring Cleanup &amp; Assessment (April)</h3>\\n\\n<p>Before you reach for the fertilizer bag, start with a thorough spring cleanup. Rake your lawn gently with a flexible leaf rake or a thatching rake to remove dead grass, fallen leaves, and debris that accumulated over winter. This process, often called <strong>power raking</strong> or <strong>dethatching</strong>, allows sunlight and air to reach the soil surface and encourages new growth.</p>\\n\\n<p>Inspect your lawn for signs of winter damage, including snow mould patches, bare spots, and areas affected by salt from roadways and sidewalks. Snow mould typically appears as circular, matted patches of grey or pink-tinged grass. Lightly rake affected areas to promote air circulation, and they will often recover on their own as temperatures warm.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Key tasks for April:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Rake the lawn to remove debris and dead grass blades</li>\\n<li>Inspect for snow mould, salt damage, and bare patches</li>\\n<li>Test your soil pH — aim for a range between 6.0 and 7.0 for optimal cool-season grass health</li>\\n<li>Sharpen mower blades and service your equipment before the first cut</li>\\n<li>Apply lime if your soil test indicates a pH below 6.0</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>Soil testing is one of the most overlooked yet impactful steps in any <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/lawn-soil-testing\\\">lawn care routine</a>. A basic soil test kit from a local garden centre will reveal your pH level and nutrient deficiencies, allowing you to make informed decisions about amendments rather than guessing.</p>\\n\\n<h3>First Mow &amp; Early Fertilization (Late April to May)</h3>\\n\\n<p>Wait until your grass reaches approximately 7 to 8 centimetres (about 3 inches) before performing the first mow of the season. Set your mower to a height of 6 to 7.5 centimetres (2.5 to 3 inches) and never remove more than one-third of the grass blade in a single cut. Leaving clippings on the lawn — a practice known as <strong>mulching</strong> — returns valuable nitrogen and organic matter to the soil.</p>\\n\\n<p>Apply a slow-release, nitrogen-rich fertilizer in mid-to-late May once the grass is actively growing. Look for a product with an NPK ratio such as 24-4-8 or 20-5-10 that includes micronutrients like iron and sulphur. Slow-release formulations feed your lawn gradually over 6 to 8 weeks, reducing the risk of burning and excessive top growth that weakens root systems.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Recommended tools &amp; products for spring:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Flexible thatching rake or power rake for dethatching</li>\\n<li>Soil test kit or professional soil analysis service</li>\\n<li>Slow-release granular fertilizer (24-4-8 or similar NPK ratio)</li>\\n<li>Rotary spreader for even fertilizer application</li>\\n<li>Sharp mower blades — dull blades tear grass and invite disease</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h3>Spring Overseeding &amp; Weed Prevention (May to Early June)</h3>\\n\\n<p>May is an excellent window for <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/overseeding-your-lawn\\\">overseeding thin or bare areas</a> of your lawn. Choose grass seed blends suited to Southern Ontario conditions. Kentucky bluegrass offers excellent density and self-repairing capabilities, while fine fescues thrive in shaded areas. For high-traffic zones, consider a blend that includes perennial ryegrass for its rapid germination and durability.</p>\\n\\n<p>Before overseeding, mow the existing lawn short and rake the soil surface to ensure good seed-to-soil contact. Spread seed at the recommended rate on the package, lightly top-dress with compost or screened topsoil, and keep the area consistently moist until seedlings are established — typically 2 to 3 weeks.</p>\\n\\n<p>For weed prevention, apply a pre-emergent herbicide in late April to early May if crabgrass has been a recurring problem. Corn gluten meal is an organic alternative that suppresses weed seed germination while adding nitrogen to the soil. However, do not apply pre-emergent products if you plan to overseed, as they will also prevent grass seed from germinating.</p>\\n\\n<h2>Summer Lawn Care in Southern Ontario (Mid-June to August)</h2>\\n\\n<p>Southern Ontario summers can be hot and humid, with temperatures regularly climbing above 30°C and occasional drought conditions. Cool-season grasses like Kentucky bluegrass, fescues, and ryegrass naturally slow their growth during peak heat, and your lawn care strategy must shift from promoting growth to protecting and sustaining the turf through environmental stress.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Mowing Strategies for Summer Heat (June to August)</h3>\\n\\n<p>One of the most important adjustments you can make during summer is raising your mower deck. Set your cutting height to 7.5 to 10 centimetres (3 to 4 inches) during hot weather. Taller grass shades the soil surface, reducing evaporation, suppressing weed germination, and keeping root zones cooler. This single practice can dramatically improve your lawn's resilience during heat waves and dry spells.</p>\\n\\n<p>Mow during the cooler parts of the day — early morning or late evening — to minimize stress on the grass. Avoid mowing when the lawn is wet from dew or irrigation, as this can lead to clumping and uneven cuts that create entry points for fungal diseases.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Watering Wisely Through Summer</h3>\\n\\n<p>Your lawn needs approximately 2.5 centimetres (1 inch) of water per week during the growing season, including rainfall. The most effective watering strategy is deep and infrequent: apply the full weekly amount in one or two sessions rather than light daily sprinklings. Deep watering encourages roots to grow downward, creating a more drought-tolerant lawn.</p>\\n\\n<p>Water early in the morning, ideally between 5:00 AM and 9:00 AM, to minimize evaporation and reduce the risk of fungal diseases that thrive on prolonged leaf wetness. Use a rain gauge or empty tuna can placed on the lawn to measure how much water your sprinkler delivers in a given time period.</p>\\n\\n<p>If water restrictions are in effect or you prefer to let your lawn go dormant during extreme heat, do not worry — cool-season grasses are adapted to survive short periods of dormancy. The grass will turn brown but will recover once cooler temperatures and rain return. The key is to avoid alternating between dormancy and active growth, which stresses the plant. Pick one strategy and stay consistent throughout the summer.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Summer Fertilization &amp; Pest Management</h3>\\n\\n<p>Avoid heavy nitrogen fertilization during the peak heat of July and August. Excessive nitrogen forces the grass to produce tender new growth that is highly susceptible to heat stress, disease, and insect damage. If your lawn shows signs of nutrient deficiency — such as yellowing or pale colour — apply a light application of a slow-release, low-nitrogen fertilizer or an iron supplement to maintain colour without pushing growth.</p>\\n\\n<p>Summer is also the season to watch for common Southern Ontario lawn pests:</p>\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Chinch bugs</strong> — small black-and-white insects that cause irregular brown patches, especially in sunny areas with Kentucky bluegrass</li>\\n<li><strong>White grubs</strong> — the larvae of Japanese beetles and June bugs that feed on grassroots, causing turf to lift easily from the soil</li>\\n<li><strong>Sod webworms</strong> — caterpillars that chew grass blades close to the crown, leaving behind small brown patches and silken tunnels</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>For grub control, apply beneficial nematodes (Heterorhabditis bacteriophora) in mid-to-late August when young grubs are actively feeding near the soil surface. Nematodes are a natural, environmentally friendly alternative to chemical insecticides and are widely available at Southern Ontario garden centres.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Recommended tools &amp; products for summer:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Oscillating or rotary sprinkler with timer for consistent watering</li>\\n<li>Rain gauge for measuring irrigation output</li>\\n<li>Beneficial nematodes for grub control</li>\\n<li>Iron-based lawn supplement for colour without excess nitrogen</li>\\n<li>Insect identification guide or local extension service resource</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Fall Lawn Care in Southern Ontario (September to November)</h2>\\n\\n<p>Fall is widely regarded by turf professionals as the <strong>most important season</strong> for lawn care in Southern Ontario. Cooler air temperatures, warm soil, and increased rainfall create ideal conditions for root growth, seed germination, and recovery from summer stress. The work you invest during these months pays dividends the following spring.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Core Aeration (Early to Mid-September)</h3>\\n\\n<p>Core aeration is the process of removing small plugs of soil from the lawn to alleviate compaction, improve water infiltration, and enhance oxygen exchange in the root zone. In Southern Ontario, where clay-heavy soils are common — particularly in newer subdivisions — annual or biennial aeration is one of the most impactful practices you can adopt.</p>\\n\\n<p>Rent a core aerator from a local equipment rental shop or hire a professional lawn care service. Make two passes over the lawn in perpendicular directions for thorough coverage. Leave the extracted soil plugs on the surface — they will break down naturally within a few weeks and return beneficial microorganisms to the thatch layer.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Fall Overseeding &amp; Top-Dressing (September)</h3>\\n\\n<p>Immediately after aerating is the perfect time to <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/best-grass-seed-for-your-lawn\\\">overseed your lawn</a>. The open holes created by aeration provide excellent seed-to-soil contact, and the warm soil temperatures of September promote rapid germination. Choose a high-quality seed blend appropriate for your site conditions:</p>\\n<ul>\\n<li><strong>Full sun areas:</strong> Kentucky bluegrass blends or Kentucky bluegrass-perennial ryegrass mixes</li>\\n<li><strong>Shaded areas:</strong> Fine fescue blends (creeping red fescue, chewings fescue)</li>\\n<li><strong>High-traffic areas:</strong> Tall fescue or perennial ryegrass blends for durability</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<p>After seeding, apply a thin layer (approximately 6 millimetres or 1/4 inch) of compost or screened topsoil as a top-dressing. This protects the seed, retains moisture, and adds organic matter to the soil profile. Water lightly and frequently — two to three times daily — until seedlings emerge, then gradually transition to deeper, less frequent watering.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Fall Fertilization (September to Late October)</h3>\\n\\n<p>Apply a high-quality fall fertilizer in early-to-mid September, approximately 4 to 6 weeks after your late-summer feeding. Look for a formulation with higher potassium content (the third number in the NPK ratio), such as 12-4-14 or 10-5-20. Potassium strengthens cell walls, improves cold hardiness, and helps the grass store energy reserves for winter survival and spring green-up.</p>\\n\\n<p>A second, lighter application in late October — often called a <strong>winterizer feeding</strong> — can further boost root development and carbohydrate storage. Use a slow-release nitrogen source to ensure nutrients remain available in the soil as long as the ground is unfrozen. This late-fall feeding is often the single most effective fertilizer application for promoting a thick, vigorous lawn the following spring.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Leaf Management &amp; Final Mow (October to November)</h3>\\n\\n<p>Fallen leaves can smother your lawn if left unattended, blocking sunlight and trapping moisture that promotes snow mould development. Rake or mulch leaves regularly throughout the fall. If you have a mulching mower, you can shred moderate amounts of leaves directly into the turf — research from several universities has shown that finely mulched leaves decompose quickly and provide beneficial organic matter without harming the grass.</p>\\n\\n<p>Continue mowing as long as the grass is actively growing. For the final cut of the season in late October or early November, lower your mower deck to approximately 5 centimetres (2 inches). Shorter grass entering winter is less prone to snow mould and vole damage, as long blades tend to mat down under snow cover and create a favourable environment for fungal pathogens.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Recommended tools &amp; products for fall:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Core aerator (rental or professional service)</li>\\n<li>High-quality grass seed blends suited to Southern Ontario</li>\\n<li>Compost or screened topsoil for top-dressing</li>\\n<li>Fall/winterizer fertilizer with elevated potassium content</li>\\n<li>Mulching mower or leaf vacuum for leaf management</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Winter Lawn Care &amp; Planning in Southern Ontario (December to March)</h2>\\n\\n<p>While your lawn is dormant during the Southern Ontario winter, there are still important practices to follow and planning steps to take that will set you up for success when spring arrives.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Protecting Your Dormant Lawn</h3>\\n\\n<p>Minimize foot traffic on frozen or snow-covered turf. Walking on frozen grass can damage the crowns and break dormant blades, leading to thin areas in spring. If you use salt or ice melt products on driveways and walkways, choose calcium chloride or calcium magnesium acetate over sodium chloride (rock salt), which is highly damaging to grass and soil structure. Create physical barriers with burlap or snow fencing near roads and sidewalks to reduce salt spray exposure.</p>\\n\\n<p>When shovelling snow, avoid piling large mounds onto lawn areas. Compacted snow takes much longer to melt in spring and can lead to prolonged soil saturation and snow mould development. Spread snow evenly across the lawn or designate a non-turf area for snow storage.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Equipment Maintenance &amp; Spring Planning</h3>\\n\\n<p>Winter is the ideal time to service your lawn care equipment. Sharpen or replace mower blades, change oil and air filters, inspect spark plugs, and clean undercarriages. Well-maintained equipment performs better, produces cleaner cuts, and reduces the risk of spreading disease from one area of the lawn to another.</p>\\n\\n<p>Review your lawn care journal or notes from the previous season. Identify problem areas — persistent weeds, drainage issues, shaded zones where grass struggles — and develop a plan to address them. Order seed, fertilizer, and any soil amendments in advance so you are ready to act as soon as conditions allow in spring.</p>\\n\\n<p><strong>Winter planning checklist:</strong></p>\\n<ul>\\n<li>Service and sharpen all mower blades and cutting equipment</li>\\n<li>Review soil test results and order amendments if needed</li>\\n<li>Research <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/common-lawn-weeds-and-how-to-control-them\\\">weed identification and control strategies</a> for the coming season</li>\\n<li>Plan any landscaping or grading projects that will improve drainage</li>\\n<li>Order grass seed early to ensure availability of preferred blends</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Southern Ontario Lawn Care</h2>\\n\\n<h3>When should I apply fertilizer to my lawn in Southern Ontario?</h3>\\n\\n<p>The optimal fertilization schedule for Southern Ontario lawns includes three to four applications per year. Apply a slow-release nitrogen fertilizer in mid-to-late May after the grass is actively growing. Follow with a light summer feeding in late June only if the lawn shows signs of nutrient deficiency. The most critical application is a potassium-rich fall fertilizer in early-to-mid September, with an optional winterizer application in late October. Always base your fertilizer choices on soil test results for the best outcomes.</p>\\n\\n<h3>What is the best grass seed for Southern Ontario lawns?</h3>\\n\\n<p>The best grass seed for Southern Ontario depends on your specific site conditions. <strong>Kentucky bluegrass</strong> is the most popular choice for full-sun lawns due to its density, colour, and self-repairing rhizomes. <strong>Fine fescues</strong> excel in shaded areas and require less water and fertilizer. <strong>Perennial ryegrass</strong> germinates quickly and handles foot traffic well, making it ideal for overseeding and high-use areas. <strong>Tall fescue</strong> offers excellent drought tolerance and disease resistance, which is increasingly valuable as summers become more variable. Many homeowners achieve the best results with a blend that combines two or more of these species. Visit our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care/choosing-the-right-grass-type\\\">guide to choosing the right grass type</a> for more detailed recommendations.</p>\\n\\n<h3>How often should I water my lawn during a Southern Ontario summer?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Aim to provide your lawn with approximately 2.5 centimetres (1 inch) of water per week during the active growing season, delivered in one or two deep watering sessions rather than frequent light sprinklings. Water early in the morning between 5:00 AM and 9:00 AM to reduce evaporation and disease risk. During extreme heat or drought, you may need to increase this amount slightly. If you choose to let your lawn go dormant, apply approximately 1.25 centimetres (1/2 inch) of water every two to three weeks to keep the crowns alive without breaking dormancy.</p>\\n\\n<h3>Should I aerate my lawn every year in Southern Ontario?</h3>\\n\\n<p>Whether you need annual aeration depends on your soil type and lawn usage. Lawns growing in heavy clay soil — which is very common throughout Southern Ontario — benefit from annual core aeration, especially in high-traffic areas where compaction occurs quickly. Lawns on sandy or loamy soils may only need aeration every two to three years. The best time to aerate in Southern Ontario is early-to-mid September when the grass is actively growing and can recover quickly. Signs that your lawn needs aeration include water pooling on the surface after rain, difficulty pushing a screwdriver into the soil, and thinning turf despite proper fertilization.</p>\\n\\n<h3>How do I deal with grubs in my Southern Ontario lawn?</h3>\\n\\n<p>White grubs — the larvae of Japanese beetles, June bugs, and European chafers — are a common problem in Southern Ontario lawns. Signs of grub damage include irregular brown patches that appear in late summer or early fall, and turf that lifts easily from the soil like a carpet because the roots have been severed. To confirm a grub infestation, cut a 30-centimetre square section of turf and peel it back. If you find more than 5 to 10 grubs per square foot, treatment is warranted. The most effective and environmentally responsible treatment is the application of beneficial nematodes (Heterorhabditis bacteriophora) in mid-to-late August when young grubs are small and feeding near the surface. Apply nematodes in the evening or on a cloudy day, irrigate the lawn before and after application, and store the product according to the manufacturer's instructions to ensure viability. For severe, recurring infestations, consult a licensed lawn care professional about targeted treatment options.</p>\\n\\n<h3>When is the best time to overseed a lawn in Southern Ontario?</h3>\\n\\n<p>The best time to overseed in Southern Ontario is <strong>late August through mid-September</strong>. During this window, soil temperatures remain warm enough to promote rapid seed germination, while cooler air temperatures and typically increased rainfall reduce heat stress on young seedlings. Fall overseeding also gives new grass several weeks to establish a strong root system before winter dormancy. Spring overseeding (late April to early May) is a secondary option, but it comes with challenges: newly germinated seedlings must survive the upcoming summer heat, and pre-emergent weed control products cannot be used simultaneously. For the highest success rate, combine fall overseeding with core aeration and top-dressing for maximum seed-to-soil contact and nutrient availability.</p>\\n\\n<p>By following this seasonal lawn care schedule tailored to Southern Ontario's climate, you will build a lawn that is thicker, greener, and more resilient year after year. Consistency and proper timing are the keys — each task builds on the previous one, creating a compounding effect that transforms an average lawn into an exceptional one. Bookmark this guide, adjust your approach based on your specific property conditions, and consult our <a href=\\\"/lawn-care\\\">complete lawn care resource library</a> for in-depth articles on every topic covered here.</p>\",\"tag\":null,\"is_online\":\"Y\",\"subtype\":null,\"slug\":null,\"cover_image\":null,\"status\":\"published\",\"created_at\":\"2026-07-29 00:27:41\"}","status":200,"url":""},"revalidate":3600,"tags":[]}